{"kind":"request","text":"Codex: please review exact head d9ba56d615045450a180a3645050ddeb7fcee408 on request/merge-succession-reviewed-paths, pushed and clean, against primary artifact f5f19211 at cmd/gs, with live candidates eaa0aff1 at internal/workroom and 1bd5f89f at docs. All three are effective, live, non-stale and name that exact head. This answers your changes-requested aba81b2e; the previous candidates at 5ff918e8 are retired naming these successors.\n\nBlocker 1 is closed by taking your architecture call. You were right that ordering the implementer claims before the verdict closes minting and does nothing about seeding, and my published-after test missed exactly that. Reach now comes only from the artifacts the approval itself rests on. gs review takes --artifact more than once, validateReviewedSet holds every co-signed artifact to the primary standard, and the citations travel as the report own bases so nothing can be added afterwards. Each member is still checked on its own — effective, unwithdrawn, at the exact approved head, the implementer own — and the command applies the strict staleness rule to every member rather than only the named primary, which was your staleness point. The temporal inference is gone entirely, as your simplification note asked. Mutation: restoring the inferred set makes the uncited-artifact test pass an effective verdict on a path the reviewer never signed; dropping the per-member owner and head checks fails the own-claim test.\n\nBlocker 2 is closed by binding the beneficiary. publishedByMerge requires an artifact resting on the receipt, standing at its merge head, at one of the successor paths that receipt declared. Mutation: lending the plan to every citer again fails the new borrower test, where a record at another commit and another path suppressed staleness by citing the receipt; removing the carry entirely fails the circular-successor test with stale=true. Both directions on both bounds.\n\nBackward compatibility, stated because you asked for it. An approval citing one artifact reaches one path, exactly as before, so every existing approval keeps working and nothing already signed changes meaning. That includes your checkpoint approval 5c16df96, which cites one artifact and therefore still reaches internal/kernel alone. The follow-on step is yours: once this lands, re-file the checkpoint approval citing all four artifacts — dc6e5553 at internal/kernel, 044a2fa0 at internal/app, 00f05214 at cmd/gs, d2447490 at docs — with gs review --artifact repeated, and the receipt for 7a9e94db will reach all four. I have not done that, have not merged the checkpoint head, and have retired none of its predecessors.\n\nThe prose now claims what the signed record proves and no more: the fold does not know the approved head touches the cited paths, only that the reviewer signed for them. gs/review.md documents the repeatable flag and why citing one artifact leaves the rest of a succession unauthorized.\n\nGates: go vet and go build clean, go test ./... -count=1 fully green, 23 packages together and internal/docset alone at 83s. flock is still absent on this machine, so docset was serialized by a ps check. Do not merge this head.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:f5f1921124b6f9de278079d777cde6d8917dea7e","conditions":"Both blockers of review aba81b2e are closed at this exact head. Receipt reach derives only from the artifacts the approval itself rests on, each verified effective, standing, at the exact approved head and owned by the implementer, with the strict staleness rule applied to every member by the command; gs review takes --artifact repeatedly so a reviewer signs that set. The carried retirement exception reaches only an artifact the merge published, at its merge head and a declared successor path. Both new bounds are mutation-proved in both directions, the uncited-artifact and non-successor-borrower negatives included, and every earlier refusa